==================================================================
DAY 9 - PART A: THEORY
Pods in Depth: Multi-container Pods, Init Containers, Lifecycle
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT A POD REALLY IS
-----------------------
A pod is a group of one or more containers that:
  - share ONE network namespace (one IP, they talk via localhost)
  - can share volumes
  - are always scheduled together on the same node
  - live and die together

Remember the "pause" container from Day 6: it holds the network
namespace; your containers join it.

Most pods have ONE container. Use multiple containers only when they
are tightly coupled helpers of the main app.


2. MULTI-CONTAINER PATTERNS
---------------------------
Sidecar     Helper that runs alongside the app for its whole life.
            Examples: log shipper, proxy (Envoy/Istio), config reloader.
Ambassador  Proxy that the app talks to for outside connections.
Adapter     Converts the app's output into a standard format
            (e.g. metrics exporter).


3. INIT CONTAINERS
------------------
Run BEFORE the app containers, one after another, each must finish
successfully (exit 0). If one fails, the kubelet retries it.

Uses:
  - wait until a database/service is reachable
  - download config or data into a shared volume
  - run a DB migration or set file permissions

  spec:
    initContainers:
    - name: wait-db
      image: busybox:1.36
      command: ["sh","-c","until nslookup db; do sleep 2; done"]
    containers:
    - name: app
      image: myapp:v1


4. NATIVE SIDECARS (Kubernetes 1.29+, GA in 1.33)
-------------------------------------------------
An init container with "restartPolicy: Always" becomes a sidecar:
it starts before the app and keeps running, and stops after it.
Older clusters simply list the sidecar as a second normal container.


5. RESTART POLICY (pod level)
-----------------------------
  Always      (default) restart containers whenever they exit
  OnFailure   restart only if exit code is not 0 (used by Jobs)
  Never       never restart

The kubelet restarts with an exponential back-off (10s, 20s, 40s ...
up to 5 min). That waiting is the "BackOff" in CrashLoopBackOff.


6. POD LIFECYCLE
----------------
Phase:          Pending -> Running -> Succeeded / Failed
Container state: Waiting / Running / Terminated
Conditions:     PodScheduled, Initialized, ContainersReady, Ready

Lifecycle hooks:
  postStart   runs right after a container starts
  preStop     runs before a container is stopped (graceful drain)

When a pod is deleted:
  1. Pod marked Terminating, removed from Service endpoints
  2. preStop hook runs (if any)
  3. SIGTERM sent to PID 1 of each container
  4. Wait terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s)
  5. SIGKILL if still running


7. STATIC PODS AND BARE PODS
----------------------------
Bare pods (like ours) are not recreated if deleted. Static pods are
created by the kubelet from files in /etc/kubernetes/manifests
(the control plane uses this). Everything else uses controllers.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Containers in a pod share IP (localhost) and can share volumes.
- Init containers run first, in order, and must succeed.
- restartPolicy + back-off explains CrashLoopBackOff.
- Delete = SIGTERM, grace period, then SIGKILL.
